WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:10.200 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Mexico's states, part 1 of 2. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10.858 --> 00:00:21.307 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We work through 16 of the Mexico regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:21.982 --> 00:00:24.617 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Baja California into view.

4
00:00:32.734 --> 00:00:49.618 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Baja California is the northern half of the long Baja peninsula at Mexico’s
far west, with the Pacific Ocean on one side and the Gulf of California on the
other, and Tijuana on the border with the United States.

5
00:00:50.276 --> 00:01:02.049 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name California comes from a Spanish romance of 1510 in which it was a
fictional island ruled by a queen called Calafia, because the first explorers
believed this peninsula really was an island.

6
00:01:02.704 --> 00:01:13.455 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the peninsula that was once thought to be an island, named after an
imaginary queen, with the wine country of Valle de Guadalupe just inland from
Tijuana.

7
00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.658 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Sonora into view.

8
00:01:24.074 --> 00:01:34.395 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Sonora fills the north-western mainland of Mexico, running from the Arizona
border in the north down to the Gulf of California in the west, with
Hermosillo in the middle of the desert.

9
00:01:35.054 --> 00:01:44.690 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It raises a large share of Mexico’s beef, and the Seri people on its coast
speak a language that is related to no other language on earth.

10
00:01:45.345 --> 00:01:56.642 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the desert state on the Gulf of California, where the tall ribbed
saguaro cactus stands in the sand and the Seri language stands alone.

11
00:01:57.346 --> 00:01:59.691 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Chihuahua into view.

12
00:02:05.085 --> 00:02:16.939 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Chihuahua is the great inland state of the north of Mexico, running south from
the border with Texas and New Mexico, with Ciudad Juárez on the Rio Grande in
the far north.

13
00:02:17.782 --> 00:02:28.231 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is the largest state in the country, bigger than the United Kingdom, and
Copper Canyon in it is deeper and larger than the Grand Canyon.

14
00:02:28.896 --> 00:02:36.013 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the biggest state, with a canyon deeper than the Grand Canyon and a
dog named after the place rather than the other way round.

15
00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.942 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Coahuila into view.

16
00:02:43.601 --> 00:02:58.531 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Coahuila sits in the north of Mexico along the Texas border, east of Chihuahua
and west of Nuevo León, with Torreón in the south-west corner by the salt
lagoon of Laguna Salada.

17
00:02:59.195 --> 00:03:09.342 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its Cuatro Ciénegas basin holds pools with species found nowhere else on
earth, which scientists study as an analogue for the earliest life on Earth.

18
00:03:09.998 --> 00:03:19.890 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the desert state with a fossil-filled pool where scientists look for
the beginning of life, and one of the longest stretches of the United States
border.

19
00:03:20.573 --> 00:03:23.986 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Nuevo León into view.

20
00:03:27.465 --> 00:03:40.817 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nuevo León lies in the interior of northern Mexico, between Coahuila to the
north and Tamaulipas to the east, with Monterrey in the south of the state
below the Sierra Madre.

21
00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:53.589 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The state was named in 1582 after the kingdom of León in Spain, and Monterrey
in it is Mexico’s industrial capital, with the highest income per head of any
large Mexican city.

22
00:03:54.251 --> 00:04:00.091 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the saddle-shaped Cerro de la Silla over the industrial capital of
the north.

23
00:04:00.771 --> 00:04:05.113 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Baja California Sur into view.

24
00:04:07.688 --> 00:04:20.146 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Baja California Sur is the southern half of the Baja peninsula at Mexico’s far
west, with La Paz on its bay and the Gulf of California along the whole
eastern shore.

25
00:04:20.821 --> 00:04:32.164 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was split off from Baja California as a state of its own in 1974, and the
whole Gulf of California along its coast is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.

26
00:04:32.814 --> 00:04:44.285 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the long thin southern half of the peninsula, the world’s aquarium on
its eastern shore and the grey whales that come there to calf in its lagoons.

27
00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:47.064 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Tamaulipas into view.

28
00:05:00.153 --> 00:05:09.836 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tamaulipas is the north-eastern state on the Gulf of Mexico, running from the
Rio Grande delta in the north down to the marshes of Veracruz in the south,
with Tampico on the coast.

29
00:05:10.493 --> 00:05:22.347 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from the Huastec word tamaholipa, roughly place where people
pray, and its Sierra de Tamaulipas reserve is where tropical, desert and
temperate country meet.

30
00:05:23.018 --> 00:05:30.646 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the place where people pray, the Gulf state whose whole border with
the United States is the Rio Grande.

31
00:05:31.409 --> 00:05:33.452 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Sinaloa into view.

32
00:05:45.582 --> 00:05:57.564 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Sinaloa runs down the Pacific coast of north-western Mexico, between Sonora in
the north and Nayarit in the south, with Culiacán inland in the northern half
of the state.

33
00:05:58.234 --> 00:06:05.827 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is Mexico’s vegetable garden, and most of the tomatoes the United States
eats in the winter months come from this coast.

34
00:06:06.476 --> 00:06:13.465 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the winter tomato coast, where the loud brass bands and the carnival
of Mazatlán both began.

35
00:06:14.307 --> 00:06:16.815 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Durango into view.

36
00:06:29.685 --> 00:06:40.308 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Durango lies in the north-west interior of Mexico, south of Chihuahua and west
of Zacatecas, with its capital of the same name in the middle of the state.

37
00:06:40.976 --> 00:06:54.885 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was named in 1563 by a Basque founder after Durango in the Basque Country,
whose own name means beyond the water, and its dry canyons have doubled for
the American West in dozens of films.

38
00:06:55.538 --> 00:07:04.327 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the state named after a Basque town, whose canyons stood in for the
Wild West and whose road runs along a knife-edge ridge.

39
00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:08.433 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Zacatecas into view.

40
00:07:23.583 --> 00:07:32.023 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Zacatecas is a small dry state in the middle of northern Mexico, with eight
neighbours crowding around it and its silver city in the middle.

41
00:07:32.676 --> 00:07:46.666 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Silver was found here in 1546, and for two centuries this one district was
among the largest silver producers in the world; the name comes from Nahuatl
words for the people of the grasslands.

42
00:07:47.326 --> 00:07:54.280 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the grassland people of the silver state, whose pink stone centre is
a UNESCO site with a cable car up the hill.

43
00:07:55.123 --> 00:07:58.269 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring San Luis Potosí into view.

44
00:08:09.789 --> 00:08:20.575 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
San Luis Potosí lies in the centre of Mexico, running from the Gulf plain in
the east up into the Sierra Madre in the west, with its capital of the same
name in the middle.

45
00:08:21.236 --> 00:08:37.868 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name is St Louis plus Potosí, borrowing the Bolivian silver mountain as a
boast that this state would be as rich, and the abandoned silver town of Real
de Catorce is reached through a tunnel of 2.3 kilometres.

46
00:08:38.545 --> 00:08:46.684 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the state that borrowed a Bolivian silver mountain for its name, with
the ghost silver town behind a long tunnel.

47
00:08:47.477 --> 00:08:50.205 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Aguascalientes into view.

48
00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:16.919 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Aguascalientes is one of the smallest states in Mexico, wedged between
Zacatecas and Jalisco in the centre of the country, with its city of hot
springs in the middle.

49
00:09:17.581 --> 00:09:30.120 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The state takes its name from the thermal springs the city was founded on in
1575, and much of the old centre is undermined by tunnels that nobody has
fully mapped.

50
00:09:30.801 --> 00:09:40.611 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the hot waters, the small state in the middle of the country, with
its underground tunnels and a fair that has run since 1828.

51
00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:43.968 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Nayarit into view.

52
00:10:02.959 --> 00:10:13.153 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nayarit is a narrow state on the Pacific, south of Sinaloa and north of
Jalisco, with Tepic in its northern half and the Sierra Madre running down its
spine.

53
00:10:13.816 --> 00:10:33.292 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is named for Nayar, a Cora chief and later a god, and the Cora resisted
Spanish rule here until 1722; the Wixárika, known as the Huichol, still live
in its sierra and are famous for their yarn paintings.

54
00:10:33.956 --> 00:10:41.456 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the narrow coastal state of the people who resisted Spain, where yarn
paintings show visions of peyote.

55
00:10:42.206 --> 00:10:44.424 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Guanajuato into view.

56
00:11:03.507 --> 00:11:12.807 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Guanajuato is a compact state in the centre of Mexico, north of Michoacán and
west of Querétaro, with León out on its western plain.

57
00:11:13.478 --> 00:11:27.898 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its silver mine La Valenciana produced perhaps two thirds of the world’s
silver in its best years, and Mexico’s war of independence began here at
Dolores Hidalgo in 1810.

58
00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:37.083 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the place of the frogs, where the independence war began with a
priest’s call to arms in the church at Dolores Hidalgo.

59
00:11:37.869 --> 00:11:40.377 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Jalisco into view.

60
00:11:56.299 --> 00:12:05.297 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Jalisco lies on the Pacific in the centre-west of Mexico, between Nayarit and
Colima, with Guadalajara on the inland plain east of the coast.

61
00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:18.673 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tequila is made by law only from blue agave grown in this state and four
others nearby, and the region also gave the world mariachi, the charreada
rodeo and the wide sombrero.

62
00:12:19.347 --> 00:12:30.260 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the state the whole world pictures as Mexico, with tequila, mariachi
and the sombrero all coming from here.

63
00:12:31.028 --> 00:12:33.408 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Querétaro into view.

64
00:12:45.477 --> 00:12:55.160 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Querétaro is a small state in the centre of Mexico, north of Michoacán and
west of Hidalgo, with its capital of the same name near the middle.

65
00:12:55.818 --> 00:13:11.085 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its aqueduct of 74 arches, built in the 1720s, still frames the city, and
Mexico’s constitution of 1917 was written here; Maximilian was shot on a hill
just outside in 1867.

66
00:13:11.745 --> 00:13:19.756 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the arches over the town, where the country wrote its constitution
and shot an emperor on the hill outside.

67
00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:51.678 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
16 regions of Mexico, one for each target of the map: Baja California, Sonora,
Chihuahua, Coahuila, Nuevo León, Baja California Sur, Tamaulipas, Sinaloa,
Durango, Zacatecas, San Luis Potosí, Aguascalientes, Nayarit, Guanajuato,
Jalisco and Querétaro.

68
00:13:52.330 --> 00:14:02.268 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the shape of Mexico from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
